COMS W4111 Project 1 Flask starter

This is the course's example Flask webserver, adapted for Python 3.14.6
and Google Cloud Run. It uses SQLAlchemy connections and explicit,
parameterized SQL, not an ORM.

Follow the Project 1 Part 3 webpage for creating your team's private
GitHub repository. Open docs/cloud-run-setup.html for the Console-only
setup instructions. These continue from the Google Cloud project,
proxy key, and PostgreSQL account already used for Part 2.

For local development, follow the assignment instructions to create a
virtual environment, install requirements.txt with pip, and create a
private .env file. Flask loads .env when you run python server.py.
Run the Cloud SQL Auth Proxy on the same computer as the local app.
Never commit .env or include it in your submission.

Commit and push the contents of this directory at the root of your team's
repository, including .python-version, .gitignore, and .gcloudignore.
Do not commit the ZIP itself, an enclosing directory, proxy-key.json,
database passwords, .env files, or local virtual environments.

Files:
  server.py              Flask routes and direct SQL examples
  requirements.txt       Python dependencies
  Procfile               Gunicorn startup command for Cloud Run
  .python-version        Python runtime selection

  templates/             Example HTML templates
  static/                Example stylesheet
  docs/cloud-run-setup.html  Setup instructions
  tests/test_server.py   Tests with mocked database connections

The starter does not create tables when imported. If the example test
table is missing, click Initialize example table on the homepage. The
button disappears once the table exists. No extra setting or deployment
is needed. Replace the example routes and templates with your own Part 3
application. Do not initialize over meaningful existing data.

This is a public teaching example, not an authenticated application.
Use invented demonstration data and never commit credentials.
